      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.766,
            0.047,
            0.862,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": "Power Play"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.089,
            0.511,
            0.113
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "2.5 Did You Ever Wonder?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.123,
            0.846,
            0.161
          ],
          "content": "Last year, we looked at interesting thought experiments in the chapter on Large Numbers. Let us continue this journey."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.16,
            0.848,
            0.199
          ],
          "content": "Nanjundappa wants to donate jaggery equal to Roxie’s weight and wheat equal to Estu’s weight. He is wondering how much it would cost."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.228,
            0.212,
            0.435,
            0.349
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_14_image_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.561,
            0.213,
            0.768,
            0.35
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_14_image_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.393,
            0.848,
            0.433
          ],
          "content": "？ What would be the worth (in rupees) of the donated jaggery? What would be the worth (in rupees) of the donated wheat?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.436,
            0.846,
            0.474
          ],
          "content": "In order to find out, let us first describe the relationships among the quantities present."
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.448,
            0.97,
            0.55,
            0.985
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.473,
            0.846,
            0.512
          ],
          "content": "Worth of jaggery (in rupees) = Roxie’s weight in kg × cost of 1 kg jaggery. Worth of wheat (in rupees) = Estu’s weight in kg × cost of 1 kg wheat."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.513,
            0.847,
            0.553
          ],
          "content": "? Make necessary and reasonable assumptions for the unknowns and find the answers. Remember, Roxie is 13 years old and Estu is 11 years old."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.556,
            0.849,
            0.632
          ],
          "content": "Assuming Roxie’s weight to be 45 kg and the cost of 1 kg of jaggery to be ₹70, the worth of donated jaggery is 45 × 70 = ₹3150. Assuming Estu’s weight to be 50 kg and the cost of 1 kg of wheat to be ₹50, the worth of donated wheat is 50 × 50 = ₹2500."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.162,
            0.655,
            0.835,
            0.733
          ],
          "content": "The practice of offering goods equal to the weight of a person, called Tulābhāra or Tulābhāram, is quite old and is still followed in many places in Southern India. It is a symbol of bhakti (surrendering oneself), <u>a token of gratitude; it also supports the community.</u>"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.752,
            0.846,
            0.791
          ],
          "content": "？ Roxie wonders, “Instead of jaggery if we use 1-rupee coins, how many coins are needed to equal my weight?”. How can we find out?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.795,
            0.847,
            0.832
          ],
          "content": "For questions like these, you can consider following the steps suggested below."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.837,
            0.847,
            0.876
          ],
          "content": "1. Guessing: Make an instinctive (quick) guess of what the answer could be, without any calculations."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.878,
            0.915,
            0.913,
            0.937
          ],
          "content": "33"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
